1. Introduction

The Hammer room turned out to be a multi‑layered challenge involving verification logic, authentication flows, JWT manipulation, file inclusion, and session handling.
Although it took more time than expected, the learning experience was extremely valuable.

2. Initial Attempt – Tools vs Manual Review

At the start, I relied heavily on automated tools.
Instead of helping, they wasted time because the room required manual logic analysis.

✔ Key Discovery

During manual review, I noticed that directories and internal content followed a specific prefix naming pattern:

hmr_

Understanding this prefix was important for navigating the challenge structure.

3. Understanding the Email Verification Flow

The next part involved analyzing the password reset / verification mechanism.

✔ Problems

I didn’t fully understand how the domain handled verification at first.

My initial script failed because I wasn’t aware of the backend rules.

✔ Rules Discovered

Through analysis and the email hint, I identified the following logic:

Verification code: 4 digits

Code is valid for 180 seconds

After 7 incorrect attempts, the session becomes invalid

Session ID resets after too many wrong tries

✔ Action Taken

I wrote a script that respected all these constraints:

Brute‑forces only within the time window

Stops at 7 attempts

Automatically handles session resets

With this, I successfully extracted the correct verification code.

4. Logging In & First Flag

After verifying the code, I logged in and obtained the first flag.
But the actual challenge began after this point.

5. JWT Analysis – Missing kid Field

The JWT looked normal, except:

✔ Observations

The kid (Key ID) was not visible in the header.

Token validation showed unusual behavior on the server.

This hinted that the backend was loading the secret from an external file.

6. Discovering File Inclusion Vulnerability

Further analysis revealed a Local File Inclusion (LFI) vulnerability connected to the JWT key loader.

✔ Key Finding

By manipulating the internal kid value, the server would load any local file as the signing key for the JWT.

This was the breakthrough needed for privilege escalation.

7. Session Persistence Problem

After logging in, the session wasn’t persistent:

It frequently expired

Every restart required repeating the verification bypass

✔ Fix

I used custom header manipulation to stabilize the session and maintain a persistent login.

8. Restricted Command Execution

The command interface only allowed one command:

ls

✔ Output

Listing the directory revealed a critical file:

188ade1.key


The filename strongly suggested it could be a secret key or something related to authentication.

9. Getting the HS256 Secret

Using the file inclusion exploit:

I forced the backend to load 188ade1.key as the JWT signing secret

Extracted the actual secret key

Crafted a new JWT with HS256 and admin privileges

✔ Steps Summary

Use LFI to include 188ade1.key

Extract the secret

Forge a valid admin token

Gain elevated access

10. Getting the Final Flag

With admin privileges unlocked, I accessed the restricted endpoint and retrieved the second flag.

11. Additional Difficulty – Machine Timeout

One of the most frustrating parts:

The machine kept timing out

Every restart required repeating:

Verification bypass

Session persistence fix

Token regeneration steps

Despite this, all obstacles were eventually solved.

🎉 Conclusion

The Hammer room offered deep practical exposure to:

OTP / verification logic analysis

Brute‑forcing with constraints

JWT exploitation using HS256

kid manipulation

Local File Inclusion (LFI)

Session hijacking & stabilization

Scripting under time‑limited conditions

A tough challenge, but absolutely worth solving.
